    "circuit": "H^n [product RZZ(theta_ij)] H^n on sorted, deduplicated unordered pairs with cyclic distance at most the specified radius. RZZ(theta)=exp(-i theta ZiZj/2). Every circuit has even-parity output support; radius 6 is the complete graph.",
    "target": "Enumerate big-endian 12-bit strings. Normalize exp(0.9*score) on even-parity strings, where score is the longest internal linear zero run bracketed by ones; strings with fewer than two ones score zero.",
    "data_rng": "default_rng(seed+7); 200 IID draws with replacement. All configurations and radii share these observations within each seed.",
    "mask_rng": "default_rng(seed+222); 512 masks with independent Bernoulli bit probability (1-exp(-1/(2*sigma^2)))/2. Resample all-zero masks and retain all other duplicates. Identical sigma and seed give identical masks across configurations and radii.",
    "initialization": "0.01*default_rng(seed+10000+7*k).standard_normal(parameter_count), assigned in sorted edge order. All objectives and learning rates on one radius share exactly the same initial angles. Shared edges across different radii need not receive the same initial value.",
    "validation_rng": "default_rng(seed+50000); 2000 additional IID target draws per seed, shared by all candidates and radii. Development validation NLL selects settings. Confirmation validation observations are diagnostic only.",
    "objectives": "Parity uses the mean squared error on the sampled empirical Walsh moments. mse averages squared probability error over the 2048 valid states; scaled-mse uses the sum of squared probability errors. All three use the same 600-update Adam implementation, with epsilon before second-moment bias correction.",
    "development_selection": "For each radius separately, select one parity setting and one MSE setting by the lowest mean validation NLL over all ten development seeds. Each family has exactly 12 candidates per radius. Use a deterministic configuration ordering for exact ties, recorded with the selection. Exact target KL is diagnostic only. These development seeds have been examined previously and provide no confirmatory evidence.",
    "confirmation_comparisons": {
      "primary": "For each of all six radii, compare selected parity with independently selected MSE, including the selected ordinary or scaled MSE objective and learning rate.",
      "secondary": "For each of all six radii, compare selected parity with ordinary probability MSE using parity's learning rate and identical initial angles, data and optimizer settings.",
      "multiplicity": "Use a fixed Bonferroni factor of 12 for the six primary and six secondary planned paired comparisons. Report two-sided Student-t intervals with individual confidence 1-0.05/12, giving approximate 95 percent family-wise coverage. If primary and secondary controls coincide, retain both labels and the factor of 12.",
      "majority_gate": "Claim parity improves KL in most tested ring architectures only if at least four of the six primary parity-minus-control adjusted interval upper bounds are strictly below zero. Always report all six architectures and both controls, including inconclusive or reversed comparisons.",
      "failures": "Retain all 120 declared seeds per architecture and every failed or nonfinite outcome. No omissions, replacements or finite probability floor. A planned comparison with any failed or nonfinite pair has an undefined interval and cannot pass its gate."
    },
    "stopping_rule": "Complete the specified development grid. Freeze selected configurations, seed list, source hashes and all comparisons before generating confirmation data. Then run this confirmation cohort once with final iterates after 600 updates. No restarts, per-dataset selection, early stopping, retuning, sample expansion or replacement confirmation cohort based on its results.",
